	'c_h1' => 'The Course',
	'c_lede' => 'Harbour Foundation is a 12-week course on Pier 3, taught in five stages from shore school to a written berth confirmation. Each intake is 18 cadets, in January, April or September.',
	'c_note' => 'Whole course: weeks 1–12',
	'c_r_b' => 'The twelve weeks change task, but the day keeps three anchors. Muster is on the quay at 07:30. On days when the simulator bridge is in use, the debrief is at 12:00. When the stage is on the water, line-handling with the tug crew is at 16:00. Shore school fills the middle of the day with charts. Harbour watch stays on the quay. Week 11 belongs to the tug and the pilot runs. The office is open Monday to Friday, 08:30 to 17:00. The college is closed on Sundays and public holidays. The first Saturday of the month is the open quay, 10:00 to 13:00, which is a visit, not a sixth training day. On a narrow screen the week is the same list, read in order, rather than a wide table.',
	'c_r_sub' => 'Weekly rhythm',
	'c_s1_b' => 'Weeks 1 to 4 are shore school. The cadet works on paper charts at the training-bridge console and learns the harbour before taking a watch: symbols, courses, and a bearing drawn in pencil. In week 4 the instructor traces the first bearing with the cadet, with the quay, the cranes and a tug visible through the bridge windows. The college keeps the charts on the table, runs the class on Pier 3, and puts a navigation instructor beside the cadet for the correction. You leave with a logbook started in your own hand and a bearing you can plot again without a prompt. The stage stays inside the 12 weeks of Harbour Foundation. It does not by itself confirm a berth.',
	'c_s2_b' => 'Weeks 5 to 7 move to the full-mission bridge simulator. The cadet stands a watch on the simulator bridge, takes a helm order, and reads the radar well enough to talk about it in the debrief. The college sets that debrief at 12:00 on simulator days, with the simulator lead in the room and the screen free of any drill that needs a printed script. You leave with a habit of saying what you saw, what you did, and what you would change, in plain words an instructor can follow. Simulator time in these three weeks is part of Harbour Foundation. It is separate from the four-day Radar / ARPA Simulator short course, which has its own fee and its own booking.',
	'c_s3_b' => 'Weeks 8 to 10 are harbour watch on the real quay. The cadet takes a line at the mooring bollards, watches the grey water and the moored vessel, and keeps a watch that can be explained back to an instructor. Week 8 is the first of these weeks on the quay. The college provides the training berth at Pier 3, the lines, and an instructor who stays in sight of the cadet. You leave with hours on the quay that were logged, and with a clearer sense of tide, traffic and the pace of a working harbour. Harbour watch is still training. A letter confirming a berth with an operator comes only after week 12, and only in writing.',
	'c_s4_b' => 'Week 11 is tug and pilot runs, a single week inside the twelve. The cadet handles lines with the tug crew, works with fenders, and sees how a pilot boarding is set up from the deck rather than from a diagram. The afternoon line-handling period is at 16:00. The college arranges the week with the harbour pilot liaison and the tug crew who train with the cadets, and keeps the work on the college’s own training routines. You leave with one logged week of tug and pilot work and a short account, in the logbook, of the runs you actually made. The college does not publish a quota of runs, and this week is not yet the assessment.',
	'c_s5_b' => 'Week 12 is assessment and berth. For the intake that begins on 12 January 2027, the assessment date is 26 March 2027 and the berth is confirmed on 2 April 2027. The berth is confirmed in writing on the Friday of week 12, after the assessment. The cadet attends the assessment and waits for the letter. The college runs the assessment, and the berth placement officer prepares the written confirmation when a berth has been arranged with an operator. You leave, when the letter is issued, with that written confirmation and with the logbook of the twelve weeks. A completed week 12 is not, by itself, a promise that every cadet will receive a berth.',
