	'college_h_r' => 'HE COLLEGE',
	'college_p1' => 'Hatoba College was founded in 1987 on Pier 3, at 4 Quay Road, Port Ellery. It opened as a maritime training college and it has stayed on that quay. From the first intake the harbour was the classroom: paper charts, lines and engines were taught in sight of cranes, tugs and moored vessels. The same idea sets the timetable now. Most cadets begin with Harbour Foundation, twelve weeks, before a rating or deck-officer preparation.',
	'college_p2' => 'The classroom is the harbour itself. In shore school an instructor and a cadet stand at the training-bridge console and trace a bearing on a paper chart, with the quay in the windows behind them. The middle weeks move to the simulator bridge, then to harbour watch, then to tug and pilot runs in week 11. After the week-12 assessment, a training berth may be a coastal ferry, a harbour tug or a short-sea cargo vessel.',
	'college_p3' => 'A cadet’s day begins at 07:30 with muster on the quay. Morning may be chart work or a watch at the berth. At 12:00 the group sits a simulator debrief and each cadet explains the decision just made. At 16:00 there is line handling with the tug crew, ropes and fenders on the quay. The office is open Monday to Friday, 08:30 to 17:00; the first Saturday of the month is an open quay, 10:00 to 13:00.',
