	'hero_lede' => 'Twelve weeks at Pier 3 take a cadet from the first bearing on a paper chart to a berth confirmed in writing. Eighteen cadets join each intake, in January, April and September, before a licence.',
	'home_go' => 'Back to the home page',
	'hs_cap' => 'Pier 3 · Four working rooms',
	'hs_h1' => 'Harbour & Simulator',
	'hs_lede' => 'Training sits on Pier 3 and in four working rooms: the full-mission bridge, the training berth, the engine room workshop, and the sea-survival pool. Visitors see the quay on the open Saturday.',
	'hs_note' => 'Four working rooms on one pier',
	'hs_o_b' => 'The office is open Monday to Friday, 08:30 to 17:00, and the college is closed on Sundays and public holidays. Training on the simulator, the berth, the workshop and the pool is scheduled inside the course day. That day begins with muster on the quay at 07:30. The simulator debrief, on days the bridge is used, is at 12:00. Line-handling with the tug crew, when the stage is on the water, is at 16:00. The time set aside for visitors is the open quay on the first Saturday of the month, 10:00 to 13:00. The next open quay is 6 February 2027. A live watch is a class. Come on the Saturday if you want to see the quay.',
	'hs_o_sub' => 'Opening hours',
	'hs_p_b' => 'The sea-survival pool is the water used for Sea Survival & Fire, the five-day short course at EUR 640. Cadets practise survival and fire drills in a pool on the college site, with an instructor present, as part of those five days. The pool is not a leisure swim and it is not open as a public session. Other programmes use it only when their own offer says so. Clothing, the drill, and the order of the day are set by the instructor at the start of the course. The college does not describe the pool by length or depth, and completion of the five days is recorded for that course alone. It does not confirm a berth on a ferry, a tug, or a cargo vessel.',
	'hs_p_sub' => 'Sea-survival pool',
	'hs_t1_b' => 'The simulator is a full-mission bridge used from week 5 to week 7 of Harbour Foundation, and again when a cadet books the four-day Radar / ARPA Simulator course. The room is dim, with wide screens showing a sea view, and the debrief looks at the radar without asking anyone to read a caption off the glass. Ines Duarte, the simulator lead, runs the watch and the debrief. On simulator days the debrief is at 12:00. The college keeps paper charts in the course as well, so the bearing work of week 4 is not dropped when the screens come on. Simulator hours are booked inside the course. They are not a public drop-in, and they are not a separate qualification sold under another name.',
	'hs_t1_i' => 'B',
	'hs_t1_r' => 'RIDGE',
	'hs_t1_sub' => 'Full-mission bridge simulator',
	'hs_t2_b' => 'The training berth is on Pier 3, at 4 Quay Road, Port Ellery. Cadets use the bollards, the lines and the small training vessel, and in week 8 they start harbour watch on this quay. The berth is also where a cadet and the berth officer meet on the gangway when a placement is being put in writing. Partner work, when it comes, is with unnamed operators of three kinds only: a coastal ferry, a harbour tug, or a short-sea cargo vessel. The college does not send cadets to a named fleet from this page. Line-handling with the tug crew, when the stage calls for it, is at 16:00. The berth remains a training berth until a Friday letter in week 12 says otherwise.',
	'hs_t2_i' => 'B',
	'hs_t2_r' => 'ERTH',
	'hs_t2_sub' => 'Pier 3 training berth',
	'hs_t3_b' => 'The engine room workshop is where engineering instruction happens, at a diesel the cadet can stand beside. Ravi Menon, the engineering instructor, teaches here. Engine Room Rating, a 10-week programme at EUR 4,200, uses the workshop as its main room. Harbour Foundation cadets meet the machinery only as far as their own stage requires, and Deck Officer Preparation uses it when the 24-week plan says so. The workshop is a teaching space on the same pier as the bridge and the berth, so a cadet can leave a chart in the morning and hear an engine in the afternoon. The college does not publish an engine rating, a horsepower, or a maker’s name for the diesel.',
