	'st3_w' => 'Weeks 8–10',
	'st4_c' => 'The cadet handles lines with the tug crew and joins the pilot runs set for that week.',
	'st4_l' => 'Stage 4',
	'st4_o' => 'The harbour pilot liaison arranges the runs with the port’s working traffic.',
	'st4_t' => 'Tug and pilot runs',
	'st4_w' => 'Week 11',
	'st5_c' => 'The cadet sits the assessment and receives the berth decision in writing on the Friday.',
	'st5_l' => 'Stage 5',
	'st5_o' => 'The college marks the week and confirms the training berth on that Friday.',
	'st5_t' => 'Assessment and berth',
	'st5_w' => 'Week 12',
	'stories_h_i' => 'C',
	'stories_h_r' => 'ADET STORIES',
	'stories_sub' => 'Three cadets',
	'story_c' => 'Noor Haddad, January 2026 cohort',
	'story_l1' => 'Elias Varga, a career change from logistics, now on a harbour tug',
	'story_l2' => 'Joana Prieto, engine room rating, on short-sea cargo',
	'story_q' => 'I came to Pier 3 for Harbour Foundation and left week 12 with a training berth on a coastal ferry. Charts in the morning, lines in the afternoon, and the written assessment on the Friday.',
	'tb_a_b' => 'Helena Brandt, the berth placement officer, arranges training berths with partner operators. The college names the work, not the company. The three kinds of operator are a coastal ferry, a harbour tug, and a short-sea cargo vessel. Noor Haddad’s berth was a coastal ferry, Elias Varga’s a harbour tug, and Joana Prieto’s a short-sea cargo ship. Those are three individual outcomes, not a menu you select at enquiry. The officer matches the programme and the assessment to a berth that an operator has actually offered for that intake. Harbour Foundation cadets reach this conversation in week 12. The college does not publish a list of fleets, a quota per operator, or a share of cadets placed.',
	'tb_a_sub' => 'How a berth is arranged',
	'tb_apply' => 'How to apply',
	'tb_c_b' => 'A berth is confirmed in writing on the Friday of week 12, after the assessment. The letter is the confirmation. For the intake that starts on 12 January 2027, the assessment is 26 March 2027 and the berth confirmation date is 2 April 2027. The same three dates are the ones the college prints on the course and on the admissions pages. The berth officer prepares the letter. The Principal is accountable for it. A handshake on the gangway may mark the day. The document is still the letter. If a berth has not been arranged, week 12 ends with the assessment record and without a berth letter. The college will say so in writing rather than leave the cadet to infer.',
	'tb_c_sub' => 'Berth confirmed',
	'tb_cap' => 'Week 12 · Berth confirmed',
	'tb_h1' => 'Training Berths',
	'tb_lede' => 'Training berths are arranged with unnamed operators of three kinds: coastal ferry, harbour tug, and short-sea cargo. Confirmation is a letter on the Friday of week 12, after assessment.',
	'tb_note' => 'Confirmed on the Friday of W12',
	'tb_t1_i' => 'A',
	'tb_t1_r' => 'RRANGED',
	'tb_t2_i' => 'C',
	'tb_t2_r' => 'ONFIRMED',
	'tb_t3_b' => 'The letter names the operator by the kind of vessel, the start of the berth, and the cadet. It does not rename the operator as a famous line, because the college does not publish company names. It does not extend the course fee, and it does not replace the medical or the eyesight test you already provided at admission. Coastal ferry, harbour tug, and short-sea cargo are different decks and different engine rooms. A cadet on Engine Room Rating is not described as a deck cadet in the letter, and a deck cadet is not described as an engine rating. Questions about a letter already issued go to the berth placement officer through admissions@hatoba-college.example.',
	'tb_t3_i' => 'T',
	'tb_t3_r' => 'HE LETTER',
	'tb_t3_sub' => 'What the letter means',
	'tb_t4_b' => 'No. Hatoba College does not confirm a training berth for every cadet who finishes, and it does not publish a placement rate. Finishing the weeks, paying the fee, and sitting the assessment are required steps, and they are still not enough for a berth letter. A berth exists when an operator has a place and the college confirms it in writing on the Friday of week 12, after the assessment. The three operator types — coastal ferry, harbour tug, short-sea cargo — are the kinds of partner the college uses. They are not an offer that one of each is waiting for every cadet. The January 2026 stories on this site are three people with three berths. They are not a promise to the next 18.',
	'tb_t4_i' => 'N',
	'tb_t4_r' => 'O PROMISE',
	'tb_t4_sub' => 'Does finishing the course confirm a berth?',
	'tb_time' => 'Time',
	'tb_what' => 'What happens',
	'vf1_t' => 'Come to 4 Quay Road, in Port Ellery, and follow the road to the water. The college does not publish a bus number, a driving time, or a station name, so this note stays with the address itself.',
	'vf2_t' => 'Pier 3 is the college quay: mooring bollards, coiled ropes, the small training vessel, and the office door on the pier. The simulator, the workshop and the pool are reached from here, not from a second gate.',
	'vf3_t' => 'The office is open Monday to Friday, 08:30 to 17:00. On the first Saturday of the month the quay is open from 10:00 to 13:00, next on 6 February 2027. On Sundays and public holidays the college is closed. If you are lost on the quay, call +00 0000 0000 during office hours.',
	'vi_a_b' => 'Hatoba College, Pier 3, 4 Quay Road, Port Ellery. Phone +00 0000 0000. Email admissions@hatoba-college.example. The office is open Monday to Friday, 08:30 to 17:00. It is closed on Sundays and on public holidays. Interviews for a place are held at this pier. Bring the medical and the eyesight test to that appointment if they are not already in the file. Fees, instalments and bursary questions are answered from this office, in writing once a place is offered. The phone number above is the published switchboard. For a named intake, email is the clearer record, because it keeps the programme and the month in one thread.',
	'vi_a_sub' => 'Address and office hours',
	'vi_cap' => 'Pier 3, 4 Quay Road',
	'vi_f_sub' => 'How to find Pier 3',
	'vi_h1' => 'Visit & Contact',
	'vi_lede' => 'The college is at Pier 3, 4 Quay Road, Port Ellery. The office is open on weekdays, and the quay is open on the first Saturday of the month.',
	'vi_note' => 'Pier 3, Port Ellery',
	'vi_q_b' => 'The open quay is the first Saturday of each month, from 10:00 to 13:00. The next open quay is Saturday 6 February 2027. Visitors and cadets are on the quay by the training vessel. You can see bollards, lines and the berth, and you can ask a practical question about the course. The open quay is not an interview and it does not hold a place among the 18. The January 2027 intake has already begun on 12 January, so a visit on 6 February will meet cadets who are in the early weeks, still in shore school. If you want a seat on a course, the later intake months to name are April and September. Weekday office hours still apply for a formal interview.',
	'vi_q_sub' => 'Open quay Saturday',
	'vi_t1_i' => 'A',
	'vi_t1_r' => 'DDRESS',
	'vi_t2_i' => 'O',
	'vi_t2_r' => 'PEN QUAY',
	'vi_t3_i' => 'F',
	'vi_t3_r' => 'IND',
	'vi_t4_i' => 'W',
	'vi_t4_r' => 'RITE',
	'vi_w_b' => 'The contact form on this page is the ordinary way to write. It asks for your name, email, phone if you wish, the intake of interest (January, April or September), a message, and consent. If the form is not available, use the Email admissions button. That opens a message to admissions@hatoba-college.example. Write the programme in the first line: Harbour Foundation, Bridge Watch Rating, Engine Room Rating, Deck Officer Preparation, Sea Survival & Fire, or Radar / ARPA Simulator. For a bursary, say so in the same message. The office reads post on weekdays between 08:30 and 17:00. A form or an email is an enquiry, which is step one. It is not yet a confirmed place.',
	'vi_w_sub' => 'Write to us',
	'wk_short' => 'W',
);
